{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 320,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey Everyone. Before we get started, \nI wanted to let you know there’s an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3280,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exciting new PBS series called Weathered: \nEarth’s Extremes as well as some new  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8560,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Merch available at the merch store. \nMore info at the end of the episode"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13440,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We know that the universe is getting \nbigger. And we know that the speed that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17160,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universe is getting bigger is also getting \nbigger. The standard assumption is that the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22960,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cosmic acceleration rate is itself is at least\nconstant, which will surely result in ultimate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28520,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "heat death of the universe. But a recent survey \nof primordial sound waves frozen into the way  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34960,
    "dDurationMs": 12440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galaxies are sprinkled through the universe \nreveals that this fate is now in question."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47400,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Back in 1998 we discovered that the expansion \nof the universe is accelerating. This rates as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52240,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one of the most important findings in the \npast several decades in physics. That’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56840,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because dark energy—our placeholder name for \nwhatever causes this acceleration—is utterly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62800,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mysterious and may be the clue we need to move \nbeyond our current understanding of physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68640,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To be clear, we do understand the math of \nhow dark energy works—just add a constant  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74200,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy density to the fabric of space and \nyou get accelerating expansion, represented  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78800,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by the cosmological constant in Einstein’s \ngeneral theory of relativity. These days,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84040,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "almost all of cosmology assumes something called \nthe Lambda-CDM model—CDM for cold dark matter,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90320,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not the topic of this video, and \nLambda for the cosmological constant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95640,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We even have a prime candidate for this \nsort of unchanging dark energy. We expect  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100200,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fluctuations in the quantum fields to \nproduce such a constant energy density,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104960,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "resulting in an “energy of the vacuum” that \nbehaves exactly like a cosmological constant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110080,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Problem is, with our current understanding \nof the quantum fields in our universe,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114640,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we would expect those quantum fluctuations \nto most naturally do one of two things:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120200,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to cancel out to exactly zero, or pile up to an \nenormous, universe-destroying value. It’s very,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127200,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very difficult to see how those \nfluctuations should produce such  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130720,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a tiny but non-zero vacuum energy without \nextreme fine-tuning of the field parameters."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136880,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are proposals for how this \nfine-tuning may have happened—for example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140880,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we could be an extremely rare universe with a \nhabitable value for the vacuum energy among a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146320,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "multitude of desolate ones. But if you \ndon’t like these anthropic arguments,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150640,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we could also reject the idea that dark energy \nis vacuum energy at all. For example it might  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156000,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be an undiscovered quantum field. One thing \nthat a new quantum field might be expected  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161200,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to do is to change in strength over time, which \nwould lead to a changing rate of acceleration."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168120,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So how would we look for a changing acceleration \nrate? Detecting that the expansion was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174240,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "accelerating at all required us to map the \nchanging size of the universe back in time.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180080,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This was done by determining the distances \nto a type of supernova—type 1a supernovae,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185080,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or exploding white dwarf stars. This approach \ngave us the expansion history over the most  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190600,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "recent half of the age of the universe. That’s \nenough to show the expansion rate is changing,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196000,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but not enough to see whether the \nchange in that rate is itself changing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200920,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We also have a data point at the \nbeginning of time with the cosmic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204840,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "microwave background radiation. If you compare \nthe expansion rates predicted by these two  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209920,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "methods assuming a constant acceleration, \nyou get a mismatch. The CMB measure gives  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215960,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an expansion rate smaller than the supernova \nmeasure. This is the now-famous crisis in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221440,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cosmology. Many believe that there’s a \nsystematic error in these measurements  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225480,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "somewhere. But another possibility is that the \nassumption of constant acceleration is wrong."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231960,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s starting to sound like it would be \na good idea to properly map the expansion  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235160,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "history back through as much of cosmic \ntime as possible with a single method.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240320,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then the acceleration history should be clear, \nand with it the constancy or non-constancy of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246560,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dark energy. Unfortunately we can’t see \nsupernovae at large enough distances,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251040,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the CMB is only at the \nfurthest distance by definition."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256000,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we do have another option: Baryon Acoustic \nOscillations—BAOs. BAOs are like standard rulers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263840,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that stretch all the way back to the beginning \nof the universe. We talked about them before,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268000,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but let me give you a refresher. In the few \nhundred thousand years right after the big bang,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272280,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the entire universe was filled with hot plasma \nand no stars or even atoms had formed. This plasma  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279520,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "flowed into the regions of higher gravity that \nwould later collapse into clusters of galaxies.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 285440,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As these massive inflows crashed together, \nvast sound waves reverberated back out,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291120,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "creating expanding shells of higher density \nmaterial. These sound waves could travel  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296320,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because matter was locked together with the \nintense radiation that filled the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301160,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But as the universe also expanded and cooled, \nand at some point the first atoms formed; matter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307280,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and light were no longer locked together and so \nour sound waves stopped moving. The pattern of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312920,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "those waves became frozen into the distribution of \nmatter at that moment a few hundred thousand years  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318720,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "after the big bang. Later, galaxies and clusters \nwould form from this matter. Most galaxies would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325120,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "form near the centers of large overdensities—but \nthere should be much weaker shells of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 331160,
    "dDurationMs": 7480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "overdensities surrounding each of these where \nthe sound waves were frozen. We see these baryon  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 338640,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "acoustic oscillations in the cosmic microwave \nbackground—the light carried to us from the moment  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 344040,
    "dDurationMs": 8040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of freezing. But we also see that there are rings \nof galaxies distantly surrounding galaxy clusters.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 352080,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is an incredibly faint signal—observable \nonly as a slightly higher probability of finding  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359040,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galaxies separated by the diameter that these \nrings have today. That diameter is called the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365720,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sound horizon of the BAOs—the maximum distance \nsound travelled before freezing—and it’s around  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372400,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "500 million light years after having \nexpanded with the rest of the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377000,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This number is our standard ruler. We know \nhow big the sound horizon should be at any  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382520,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "point in the history of the expanding universe. \nBy measuring the angular size of this horizon on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 388360,
    "dDurationMs": 8320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the sky, we can find the true physical distance \ncorresponding to the galaxies on these BAO rings.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396680,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With one more ingredient, that translates to an \nexpansion history of the universe. That final  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402440,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ingredient is redshift, and this is exactly what \nthe Dark Energy Spectroscopic Instrument measures."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410040,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "DESI is a pretty amazing instrument and survey. \nIt measures redshifts—the amount that light is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416200,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stretched as it travels to us through an expanding \nuniverse. We’ve been able to measure redshifts  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 421440,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "since the 20’s—that’s how we discovered the \nexpansion of the universe in the first place. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426880,
    "dDurationMs": 8280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "DESI is able to measure redshifts of 5000 galaxies \nor quasars at a time. For every patch of the sky,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 435160,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "5000 robots place as many optical fibers at \nprecise positions on a plate, corresponding  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 440240,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the precise locations of galaxies in that \npatch. Light from those galaxies travels the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445480,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "optical fiber down to a spectrograph, where it’s \nsplit into component wavelengths. Characteristic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451080,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spikes in emission due to known elements \nin each galaxy then reveal the redshifts."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 456760,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In this way, DESI has been able to measure \nredshifts for 26 million galaxies and quasars  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461920,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so far, and the plan is to get to 40 million by \nthe end of the survey. This will ultimately result  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467400,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a giant three dimensional map of the nearby \nuniverse over the past 11 billion years. Combined  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 474480,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with the size of the BAO rings at each redshift, \nthis also gives us a map of the expansion history."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481400,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Back in April of this year, the DESI collaboration \npublished a series of papers with the results from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486280,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the first year of the survey—from those 6 million \nredshifts. One of those papers covered the baryon  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 491480,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "acoustic oscillations, in which they determine \nconstraints on a few cosmological parameters. One  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 497720,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of these is something called omega, which gives \nthe so-called “equation of state” of dark energy.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 503720,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It tells us how much pressure is being produced \nfor a given energy density. In Lambda CDM,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509560,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we expect omega to equal exactly -1 for all \nof time, and the DESI results are in very  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516760,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "good agreement with omega = -1 and constant \ndark energy, just as we have always assumed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523960,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That sounds anticlimactic, but then something \nodd turned up. The DESI team combined the sound  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530480,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horizon measurements from their redshift survey \nwith sound horizon measurements from the two  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535560,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other types of survey: from the Planck \nsatellite measurements of the cosmic microwave  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539640,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "background at the beginning of time, and with a \nfew supernova surveys of the more modern universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545760,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Adding these other surveys led to an unexpected \nresult. The combined data were better fit with a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 553000,
    "dDurationMs": 9360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "model with omega \u003e -1 and in which dark energy \nis getting weaker over time. And that’s the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 562360,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "case whether the CMB or supernova results \nwere added to DESI separately or together.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568000,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The statistical significance of this result \ndepends on which combination of datasets you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571680,
    "dDurationMs": 8040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "look at. It’s 2.6-sigma for DESI + CMB, and \nup to nearly 4-sigma with a particular one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579720,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the supernova samples included. 2.6-sigma \nis considered not statistically significant,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585640,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "although still worthy of curiosity and more \nobservations. 4-sigma is getting exciting,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591720,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even if it’s short of the normal 5-sigma \nthreshold for a very likely positive detection."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 597840,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we should also be quite wary of that 4-sigma \nresult because it depends on inferring distances  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 603800,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from supernovae. The BAO sound horizons measured \nthis way are probably more subject to systematic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609720,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "errors than are the CMB results. That’s because \nsupernova distances depend on a longer chain of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615920,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distance measurements, and so there are more \nplaces that some error could have crept in."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620360,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, let’s allow ourselves to get excited \njust for a minute and ask what this means  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625200,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if it’s true. One of the more general models for \nnon-constant dark energy is called “quintessence”,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630520,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which has an equation of state that can change \nover time. The DESI paper focuses on a version  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635960,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of this stuff called thawing quintessence, in \nwhich an initially constant dark energy sort  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641920,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of defrosts, becoming variable over time. And \nDesi finds this model is more consistent with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648760,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the various combined surveys than constant dark \nenergy. If dark energy behaves this way then  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653880,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s less likely that it will reverse course \nand start to get stronger in the future. That  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658640,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may preclude the Big Rip scenario, in which dark \nenergy gets so powerful that it shreds the fabric  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664320,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of space time. So, the universe hopefully \ndoesn’t end as we described in this episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669960,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The opposite extreme to the big rip is the big \ncrunch, in which the expansion eventually slows,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 675880,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then turns around and the universe collapses \ninto a reverse big bang—potentially even  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680640,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "kickstarting a new universe. We don’t \nexpect that to happen even with no dark  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685720,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy whatsoever. The current expansion rate is \nenough to keep the universe expanding forever,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 691000,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even ignoring dark energy and including the \nslow-down from the inward gravitational pull  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696520,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of all galaxies. If the density of dark energy is \ndiminishing and eventually hits zero, that won’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702200,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stop eternal expansion—although it will hold off \nthe accompanying heat death for a while longer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 708720,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To get a big crunch, dark energy would have to \nchange in a more fundamental way—it would have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712760,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to shift to a positive gravitational force \nrather than a negative one. And that’s not  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717760,
    "dDurationMs": 8280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "impossible. If the equation of state parameter \nomega also changes, and in particular rises from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726040,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the current -1 to -⅓ or higher then dark energy \nstops behaving like dark energy at all and could  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734440,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "work to recollapse the universe. Then we might get \na proper crunchy end to the universe after all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741160,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But perhaps the most exciting outcome of \ndiscovering a non-constant dark energy is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746280,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that it may constrain other physics. We’ve \nbeen a bit stuck this past few decades,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752200,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unable to move beyond general relativity and the \nstandard model of particle physics. Things like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756720,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "string theory seemed promising, but have trouble \ngenerating testable predictions. String theory  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761760,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is especially challenging because it predicts a \nmyriad of possible universes called the string  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 766640,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "landscape and it seems impossible to determine how \nto identify our universe in this space. Well, in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 773840,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "2018 a team of theoretical physicists showed that \nonly a subset of the string landscape should lead  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780320,
    "dDurationMs": 8600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to stable universes, and that a requirement of \nthat stability of that subset is that dark energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 788920,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "should diminish over time. So perhaps we have \nour first tested prediction of string theory and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795360,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "string theorists are going to be pretty excited \nif the DESI result pans out. OK, one final word.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 801400,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mentioned the crisis in cosmology—the tension \nbetween the CMB versus supernova measurements  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806520,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the expansion rate as measured by the Hubble \nconstant. This Hubble tension could be resolved  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 812240,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with a changing acceleration rate. Unfortunately, \nthe acceleration rate would have to increase to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818400,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "resolve the tension, not decrease as DESI is \nsuggesting. In fact the DESI results do also infer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 826560,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an expansion rate the hubble constant—and they \nactually support the Planck CMB result and not the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 832520,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "supernova result. So it seems we have to be tense \nabout the Hubble constant for a while longer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 838880,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And once again, please remember the this \nresult has not yet reached the threshold  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 843960,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of statistical significance that we need for \na confident declaration of a discovery. We’ll  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849800,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "learn more soon though. DESI has actually \ncompleted its second year of observations,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854520,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and its results will come out \nfaster than the first year results,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857760,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so we’ll see if the finding firms up \nor evaporates. And in a few years,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863920,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when DESI has observed all 40 million galaxies \nand quasars, and we have results from other  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 870240,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "current or future programs such as the Dark Energy \nSurvey and the Vera Rubin observatory, we will  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876080,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gradually be able to hone in on a more precise \nvalue of the equation of state of dark energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 881880,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And then we should know one way or another—do we \nhave a cosmological constant or a cosmological  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888800,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "variable. And perhaps we’ll finally \nunderstand what dark energy actually is,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 893560,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and have evidence for the underlying \ntheory of everything that supports this,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 897000,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and even know the final fate \nof our expanding space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 901920,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hi Everyone, before we go I wanted to tell you \nabout an exciting new PBS long form documentary  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 907080,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "series, “Weathered: Earth's Extremes.” This \nsix part, 30 minute per episode series is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 912840,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an evolution of the @PBSTerra YouTube series \nand rigorously examines the data to understand  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 920160,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how our climate is shifting. In the series, \nhost Maiya May meets with the scientists and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 926720,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "communities on the front lines of our changing \nweather experiences and explores the actionable  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 932240,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "steps we can take to develop a more resilient \nfuture. All episodes will be available globally  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 938200,
    "dDurationMs": 7880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on PBS.org as well as on PBS app and streaming \nplatforms in the US. The first long form episode,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 946080,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "focusing on climate tipping points, out \nnow and there’s links in the description."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 952160,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I also wanted to tell you about our latest UV Glow \nT-Shirt and Hoodie. As you know, one of the long  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 957400,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "standing rules of PBS Space Time is that “It’s \nnever aliens,” but the full rule is that “It’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 963360,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "never aliens, until it is.” And in case you happen \nto run into aliens and want to communicate that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 969560,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you’re open to leaving Earth, then take a look at \nour Take Me With You UV Glow T-Shirt and Hoodie.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 975680,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Both the t-shirt and hoodie are UV charged, so \nafter being out in the sun they’ll begin to glow  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 981080,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is perfect for signaling aliens in the dark. \nBecause of the UV printing process, we’re only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 986640,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "able to print a limited number. The shirts will be \non sale until October 31st, but If you’d like to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 992960,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "receive your shirt before Halloween, then you’ll \nneed to put your order in by October 16th.We’re  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 999200,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also extending the sale of our Universal Speed \nLimit Patch \u0026 Lightcone Pin until October 13th  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1005880,
    "dDurationMs": 12960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Check out pbsspacetime.com/shop to check them \nout. There’s a link the in the description."
    } ]
  } ]
}
